{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CCjhPXiX8aBpSwiNd1Uz1C","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule Abyss.Telemetry do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  The following telemetry spans and metrics are emitted by abyss\n     4→\n     5→  ## Telemetry Metrics\n     6→\n     7→  In addition to span events, Abyss provides real-time metrics tracking through\n     8→  the `Abyss.Telemetry` module:\n     9→\n    10→  ### Connection Metrics\n    11→  - `connections_active`: Number of currently active connections\n    12→  - `connections_total`: Total number of connections since server start\n    13→  - `accepts_total`: Total number of accepted connections\n    14→  - `responses_total`: Total number of responses sent\n    15→  - `accepts_per_second`: Current accepts per second rate\n    16→  - `responses_per_second`: Current responses per second rate\n    17→\n    18→  ### Response Time Metrics\n    19→  - `[:abyss, :metrics, :response_time]`: Event emitted for each response with timing\n    20→\n    21→  ### Using Metrics\n    22→  ```elixir\n    23→  # Get current metrics\n    24→  metrics = Abyss.Telemetry.get_metrics()\n    25→  # => %{\n    26→  #   connections_active: 15,\n    27→  #   connections_total: 1250,\n    28→  #   accepts_total: 1250,\n    29→  #   responses_total: 1198,\n    30→  #   accepts_per_second: 25,\n    31→  #   responses_per_second: 23\n    32→  # }\n    33→\n    34→  # Reset all metrics\n    35→  Abyss.Telemetry.reset_metrics()\n    36→\n    37→  # Listen for response time events\n    38→  :telemetry.attach_many(\n    39→    \"response-time-listener\",\n    40→    [[:abyss, :metrics, :response_time]],\n    41→    &handle_response_time/4,\n    42→    %{}\n    43→  )\n    44→  ```\n    45→\n    46→  ## Telemetry Spans\n    47→\n    48→  The following telemetry spans are emitted by abyss\n    49→\n    50→  ## `[:abyss, :listener, *]`\n    51→\n    52→  Represents a Abyss server listening to a port\n    53→\n    54→  This span is started by the following event:\n    55→\n    56→  * `[:abyss, :listener, :start]`\n    57→\n    58→      Represents the start of the span\n    59→\n    60→      This event contains the following measurements:\n    61→\n    62→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n    63→\n    64→      This event contains the following metadata:\n    65→\n    66→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n    67→      * `local_address`: The IP address that the listener is bound to\n    68→      * `local_port`: The port that the listener is bound to\n    69→      * `transport_module`: The transport module in use\n    70→      * `transport_options`: Options passed to the transport module at startup\n    71→\n    72→\n    73→  This span is ended by the following event:\n    74→\n    75→  * `[:abyss, :listener, :stop]`\n    76→\n    77→      Represents the end of the span\n    78→\n    79→      This event contains the following measurements:\n    80→\n    81→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n    82→      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n    83→\n    84→      This event contains the following metadata:\n    85→\n    86→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n    87→      * `local_address`: The IP address that the listener is bound to\n    88→      * `local_port`: The port that the listener is bound to\n    89→      * `transport_module`: The transport module in use\n    90→      * `transport_options`: Options passed to the transport module at startup\n    91→\n    92→  ## `[:abyss, :acceptor, *]`\n    93→\n    94→  Represents a Abyss acceptor process listening for connections\n    95→\n    96→  This span is started by the following event:\n    97→\n    98→  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :start]`\n    99→\n   100→      Represents the start of the span\n   101→\n   102→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   103→\n   104→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   105→\n   106→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   107→\n   108→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   109→      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:listener` which created this acceptor\n   110→\n   111→  This span is ended by the following event:\n   112→\n   113→  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :stop]`\n   114→\n   115→      Represents the end of the span\n   116→\n   117→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   118→\n   119→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   120→      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n   121→      * `connections`: The number of client requests that the acceptor handled\n   122→\n   123→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   124→\n   125→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   126→      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:listener` which created this acceptor\n   127→      * `error`: The error that caused the span to end, if it ended in error\n   128→\n   129→  The following events may be emitted within this span:\n   130→\n   131→  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :spawn_error]`\n   132→\n   133→      Abyss was unable to spawn a process to handle a connection. This occurs when too\n   134→      many connections are in progress; you may want to look at increasing the `num_connections`\n   135→      configuration parameter\n   136→\n   137→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   138→\n   139→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   140→\n   141→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   142→\n   143→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   144→\n   145→  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :econnaborted]`\n   146→\n   147→      Abyss was unable to spawn a process to handle a connection since the remote end\n   148→      closed before we could accept it. This usually occurs when it takes too long for your server\n   149→      to start processing a connection; you may want to look at tuning OS-level TCP parameters or\n   150→      adding more server capacity.\n   151→\n   152→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   153→\n   154→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   155→\n   156→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   157→\n   158→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   159→\n   160→  ## `[:abyss, :connection, *]`\n   161→\n   162→  Represents Abyss handling a specific client request\n   163→\n   164→  This span is started by the following event:\n   165→\n   166→  * `[:abyss, :connection, :start]`\n   167→\n   168→      Represents the start of the span\n   169→\n   170→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   171→\n   172→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   173→\n   174→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   175→\n   176→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   177→      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:acceptor` span which accepted\n   178→      this connection\n   179→      * `remote_address`: The IP address of the connected client\n   180→      * `remote_port`: The port of the connected client\n   181→\n   182→  This span is ended by the following event:\n   183→\n   184→  * `[:abyss, :connection, :stop]`\n   185→\n   186→      Represents the end of the span\n   187→\n   188→      This event contains the following measurements:\n   189→\n   190→      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n   191→      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n   192→      * `send_oct`: The number of octets sent on the connection\n   193→      * `send_cnt`: The number of packets sent on the connection\n   194→      * `recv_oct`: The number of octets received on the connection\n   195→      * `recv_cnt`: The number of packets received on the connection\n   196→\n   197→      This event contains the following metadata:\n   198→\n   199→      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n   200→      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:acceptor` span which accepted\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7KgdziV3oR5cphfXQGxxs","session_id":"50d21925-9237-40c9-8dd2-03e5c6e35c37","uuid":"807ffd90-6c74-4c6d-95a1-dcf9a6097044","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/abyss/lib/abyss/telemetry.ex","content":"defmodule Abyss.Telemetry do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  The following telemetry spans and metrics are emitted by abyss\n\n  ## Telemetry Metrics\n\n  In addition to span events, Abyss provides real-time metrics tracking through\n  the `Abyss.Telemetry` module:\n\n  ### Connection Metrics\n  - `connections_active`: Number of currently active connections\n  - `connections_total`: Total number of connections since server start\n  - `accepts_total`: Total number of accepted connections\n  - `responses_total`: Total number of responses sent\n  - `accepts_per_second`: Current accepts per second rate\n  - `responses_per_second`: Current responses per second rate\n\n  ### Response Time Metrics\n  - `[:abyss, :metrics, :response_time]`: Event emitted for each response with timing\n\n  ### Using Metrics\n  ```elixir\n  # Get current metrics\n  metrics = Abyss.Telemetry.get_metrics()\n  # => %{\n  #   connections_active: 15,\n  #   connections_total: 1250,\n  #   accepts_total: 1250,\n  #   responses_total: 1198,\n  #   accepts_per_second: 25,\n  #   responses_per_second: 23\n  # }\n\n  # Reset all metrics\n  Abyss.Telemetry.reset_metrics()\n\n  # Listen for response time events\n  :telemetry.attach_many(\n    \"response-time-listener\",\n    [[:abyss, :metrics, :response_time]],\n    &handle_response_time/4,\n    %{}\n  )\n  ```\n\n  ## Telemetry Spans\n\n  The following telemetry spans are emitted by abyss\n\n  ## `[:abyss, :listener, *]`\n\n  Represents a Abyss server listening to a port\n\n  This span is started by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :listener, :start]`\n\n      Represents the start of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `local_address`: The IP address that the listener is bound to\n      * `local_port`: The port that the listener is bound to\n      * `transport_module`: The transport module in use\n      * `transport_options`: Options passed to the transport module at startup\n\n\n  This span is ended by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :listener, :stop]`\n\n      Represents the end of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `local_address`: The IP address that the listener is bound to\n      * `local_port`: The port that the listener is bound to\n      * `transport_module`: The transport module in use\n      * `transport_options`: Options passed to the transport module at startup\n\n  ## `[:abyss, :acceptor, *]`\n\n  Represents a Abyss acceptor process listening for connections\n\n  This span is started by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :start]`\n\n      Represents the start of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:listener` which created this acceptor\n\n  This span is ended by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :stop]`\n\n      Represents the end of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n      * `connections`: The number of client requests that the acceptor handled\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:listener` which created this acceptor\n      * `error`: The error that caused the span to end, if it ended in error\n\n  The following events may be emitted within this span:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :spawn_error]`\n\n      Abyss was unable to spawn a process to handle a connection. This occurs when too\n      many connections are in progress; you may want to look at increasing the `num_connections`\n      configuration parameter\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n\n  * `[:abyss, :acceptor, :econnaborted]`\n\n      Abyss was unable to spawn a process to handle a connection since the remote end\n      closed before we could accept it. This usually occurs when it takes too long for your server\n      to start processing a connection; you may want to look at tuning OS-level TCP parameters or\n      adding more server capacity.\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n\n  ## `[:abyss, :connection, *]`\n\n  Represents Abyss handling a specific client request\n\n  This span is started by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :connection, :start]`\n\n      Represents the start of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:acceptor` span which accepted\n      this connection\n      * `remote_address`: The IP address of the connected client\n      * `remote_port`: The port of the connected client\n\n  This span is ended by the following event:\n\n  * `[:abyss, :connection, :stop]`\n\n      Represents the end of the span\n\n      This event contains the following measurements:\n\n      * `monotonic_time`: The time of this event, in `:native` units\n      * `duration`: The span duration, in `:native` units\n      * `send_oct`: The number of octets sent on the connection\n      * `send_cnt`: The number of packets sent on the connection\n      * `recv_oct`: The number of octets received on the connection\n      * `recv_cnt`: The number of packets received on the connection\n\n      This event contains the following metadata:\n\n      * `telemetry_span_context`: A unique identifier for this span\n      * `parent_telemetry_span_context`: The span context of the `:acceptor` span which accepted","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":845}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JEaqLMfXacqk6RfoYcPQaL","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule Abyss.Transport.UDP.Core do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Core UDP transport functionality shared between Unicast and Broadcast transports.\n     4→\n     5→  This module contains common UDP socket operations that are used by both\n     6→  `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast` and `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` to avoid\n     7→  code duplication.\n     8→\n     9→  ## Shared Operations\n    10→\n    11→  - Socket control and ownership transfer\n    12→  - Data receiving and sending\n    13→  - Socket option management\n    14→  - Socket information retrieval\n    15→  - Connection statistics\n    16→\n    17→  This module is not meant to be used directly. Use the specific transport\n    18→  modules instead:\n    19→  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast` for unicast traffic\n    20→  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` for broadcast/multicast traffic\n    21→  \"\"\"\n    22→\n    23→  @doc \"\"\"\n    24→  Transfers ownership of the given socket to the given process.\n    25→  \"\"\"\n    26→  @spec controlling_process(Abyss.Transport.socket(), pid()) ::\n    27→          Abyss.Transport.on_controlling_process()\n    28→  defdelegate controlling_process(socket, pid), to: :gen_udp\n    29→\n    30→  @doc \"\"\"\n    31→  Receives data from a UDP socket.\n    32→  \"\"\"\n    33→  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer(), timeout()) ::\n    34→          Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n    35→  defdelegate recv(socket, length, timeout), to: :gen_udp\n    36→\n    37→  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n    38→  defdelegate recv(socket, length), to: :gen_udp\n    39→\n    40→  @doc \"\"\"\n    41→  Sends data on a UDP socket.\n    42→  \"\"\"\n    43→  @spec send(Abyss.Transport.socket(), iodata()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_send()\n    44→  defdelegate send(socket, data), to: :gen_udp\n    45→  defdelegate send(socket, dest, data), to: :gen_udp\n    46→  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, data), to: :gen_udp\n    47→  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, anc_data, data), to: :gen_udp\n    48→\n    49→  @doc \"\"\"\n    50→  Gets socket options.\n    51→  \"\"\"\n    52→  @spec getopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_get_options()) ::\n    53→          Abyss.Transport.on_getopts()\n    54→  defdelegate getopts(socket, options), to: :inet\n    55→\n    56→  @doc \"\"\"\n    57→  Sets socket options.\n    58→  \"\"\"\n    59→  @spec setopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_set_options()) ::\n    60→          Abyss.Transport.on_setopts()\n    61→  defdelegate setopts(socket, options), to: :inet\n    62→\n    63→  @doc \"\"\"\n    64→  Closes a UDP socket.\n    65→  \"\"\"\n    66→  @spec close(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) :: :ok\n    67→  defdelegate close(socket), to: :gen_udp\n    68→\n    69→  @doc \"\"\"\n    70→  Returns information about the local socket endpoint.\n    71→  \"\"\"\n    72→  @spec sockname(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) ::\n    73→          Abyss.Transport.on_sockname()\n    74→  defdelegate sockname(socket), to: :inet\n    75→\n    76→  @doc \"\"\"\n    77→  Returns information about the remote socket endpoint.\n    78→  \"\"\"\n    79→  @spec peername(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_peername()\n    80→  defdelegate peername(socket), to: :inet\n    81→\n    82→  @doc \"\"\"\n    83→  Returns statistics about the socket connection.\n    84→  \"\"\"\n    85→  @spec getstat(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.socket_stats()\n    86→  defdelegate getstat(socket), to: :inet\n    87→\n    88→  @doc \"\"\"\n    89→  Merges user options with default options, ensuring user options take precedence.\n    90→\n    91→  The function handles both keyword-style options (e.g., `{:key, value}`) and\n    92→  atom-style options (e.g., `:atom_option`).\n    93→\n    94→  ## Parameters\n    95→  - `default_options` - Default options to use as base\n    96→  - `user_options` - User-provided options that override defaults\n    97→\n    98→  ## Returns\n    99→  - Merged options list with user options taking precedence\n   100→  \"\"\"\n   101→  @spec merge_options(keyword(), keyword()) :: keyword()\n   102→  def merge_options(default_options, user_options) do\n   103→    Enum.uniq_by(\n   104→      user_options ++ default_options,\n   105→      fn\n   106→        {key, _} when is_atom(key) -> key\n   107→        key when is_atom(key) -> key\n   108→      end\n   109→    )\n   110→  end\n   111→\n   112→  @doc \"\"\"\n   113→  Opens a UDP socket with the given port and options.\n   114→\n   115→  This is a common helper used by both Unicast and Broadcast transports.\n   116→  \"\"\"\n   117→  @spec open_socket(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n   118→          {:ok, Abyss.Transport.socket()} | {:error, :system_limit} | {:error, :inet.posix()}\n   119→  def open_socket(port, options) do\n   120→    :gen_udp.open(port, options)\n   121→  end\n   122→end\n   123→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7KgdziV3oR5cphfXQGxxs","session_id":"50d21925-9237-40c9-8dd2-03e5c6e35c37","uuid":"33930d9f-9fbf-4e9c-9359-30995d4ddee9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/abyss/lib/abyss/transport/udp/core.ex","content":"defmodule Abyss.Transport.UDP.Core do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Core UDP transport functionality shared between Unicast and Broadcast transports.\n\n  This module contains common UDP socket operations that are used by both\n  `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast` and `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` to avoid\n  code duplication.\n\n  ## Shared Operations\n\n  - Socket control and ownership transfer\n  - Data receiving and sending\n  - Socket option management\n  - Socket information retrieval\n  - Connection statistics\n\n  This module is not meant to be used directly. Use the specific transport\n  modules instead:\n  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast` for unicast traffic\n  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` for broadcast/multicast traffic\n  \"\"\"\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Transfers ownership of the given socket to the given process.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec controlling_process(Abyss.Transport.socket(), pid()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_controlling_process()\n  defdelegate controlling_process(socket, pid), to: :gen_udp\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Receives data from a UDP socket.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer(), timeout()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n  defdelegate recv(socket, length, timeout), to: :gen_udp\n\n  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n  defdelegate recv(socket, length), to: :gen_udp\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Sends data on a UDP socket.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec send(Abyss.Transport.socket(), iodata()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_send()\n  defdelegate send(socket, data), to: :gen_udp\n  defdelegate send(socket, dest, data), to: :gen_udp\n  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, data), to: :gen_udp\n  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, anc_data, data), to: :gen_udp\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Gets socket options.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec getopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_get_options()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_getopts()\n  defdelegate getopts(socket, options), to: :inet\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Sets socket options.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec setopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_set_options()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_setopts()\n  defdelegate setopts(socket, options), to: :inet\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Closes a UDP socket.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec close(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) :: :ok\n  defdelegate close(socket), to: :gen_udp\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Returns information about the local socket endpoint.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec sockname(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_sockname()\n  defdelegate sockname(socket), to: :inet\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Returns information about the remote socket endpoint.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec peername(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_peername()\n  defdelegate peername(socket), to: :inet\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Returns statistics about the socket connection.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec getstat(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.socket_stats()\n  defdelegate getstat(socket), to: :inet\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Merges user options with default options, ensuring user options take precedence.\n\n  The function handles both keyword-style options (e.g., `{:key, value}`) and\n  atom-style options (e.g., `:atom_option`).\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `default_options` - Default options to use as base\n  - `user_options` - User-provided options that override defaults\n\n  ## Returns\n  - Merged options list with user options taking precedence\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec merge_options(keyword(), keyword()) :: keyword()\n  def merge_options(default_options, user_options) do\n    Enum.uniq_by(\n      user_options ++ default_options,\n      fn\n        {key, _} when is_atom(key) -> key\n        key when is_atom(key) -> key\n      end\n    )\n  end\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Opens a UDP socket with the given port and options.\n\n  This is a common helper used by both Unicast and Broadcast transports.\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec open_socket(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n          {:ok, Abyss.Transport.socket()} | {:error, :system_limit} | {:error, :inet.posix()}\n  def open_socket(port, options) do\n    :gen_udp.open(port, options)\n  end\nend\n","numLines":123,"startLine":1,"totalLines":123}}}
