{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vv26npsBm96D75g5nbHUry","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  UDP transport implementation for unicast traffic.\n     4→\n     5→  This transport is optimized for standard unicast UDP communication patterns,\n     6→  such as DNS queries, DHCP unicast messages, and other point-to-point UDP protocols.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Characteristics\n     9→\n    10→  - Socket configured with `active: false` for passive receive mode\n    11→  - Broadcast disabled (`broadcast: false`)\n    12→  - Optimized for request/response patterns\n    13→  - Multiple listener processes for load distribution\n    14→  - Connection pooling support\n    15→\n    16→  ## Usage\n    17→\n    18→  ```elixir\n    19→  Abyss.start_link([\n    20→    transport_module: Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast,\n    21→    handler_module: MyUnicastHandler,\n    22→    port: 53,\n    23→    num_listeners: 50\n    24→  ])\n    25→  ```\n    26→\n    27→  ## Handler Requirements\n    28→\n    29→  Handlers used with this transport should implement the `Abyss.Handler` behaviour\n    30→  and be designed for unicast request/response patterns.\n    31→\n    32→  ## Default Options\n    33→\n    34→  The following default options are set for unicast traffic:\n    35→  - `mode: :binary` - Binary mode for data\n    36→  - `reuseaddr: true` - Allow address reuse\n    37→  - `reuseport: true` - Allow port reuse across listeners\n    38→  - `active: false` - Passive receive mode\n    39→  - `broadcast: false` - Disable broadcast\n    40→\n    41→  ## See Also\n    42→\n    43→  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` - For broadcast/multicast traffic\n    44→  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP` - Original unified UDP transport (backward compatibility)\n    45→  \"\"\"\n    46→\n    47→  @behaviour Abyss.Transport\n    48→\n    49→  alias Abyss.Transport.UDP.Core\n    50→\n    51→  @hardcoded_options [\n    52→    mode: :binary,\n    53→    reuseaddr: true,\n    54→    reuseport: true,\n    55→    active: false,\n    56→    broadcast: false\n    57→  ]\n    58→\n    59→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n    60→  @doc \"\"\"\n    61→  Creates and returns a listener socket for unicast UDP traffic.\n    62→\n    63→  ## Parameters\n    64→  - `port` - The UDP port to listen on\n    65→  - `user_options` - Additional socket options provided by the user\n    66→\n    67→  ## Returns\n    68→  - `{:ok, socket}` - Successfully opened socket\n    69→  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to open socket\n    70→\n    71→  ## Examples\n    72→\n    73→      iex> Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast.listen(5353, [ip: {0, 0, 0, 0}])\n    74→      {:ok, #Port<0.1234>}\n    75→  \"\"\"\n    76→  @spec listen(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n    77→          Abyss.Transport.on_listen()\n    78→  def listen(port, user_options) do\n    79→    default_options = []\n    80→\n    81→    resolved_options = Core.merge_options(@hardcoded_options ++ default_options, user_options)\n    82→\n    83→    Core.open_socket(port, resolved_options)\n    84→  end\n    85→\n    86→  @doc \"\"\"\n    87→  Opens a UDP socket for sending unicast traffic.\n    88→\n    89→  This is typically used for creating client sockets.\n    90→\n    91→  ## Parameters\n    92→  - `port` - The local UDP port (use 0 for any available port)\n    93→  - `user_options` - Additional socket options\n    94→\n    95→  ## Returns\n    96→  - `{:ok, socket}` - Successfully opened socket\n    97→  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to open socket\n    98→  \"\"\"\n    99→  @spec open(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n   100→          Abyss.Transport.on_open()\n   101→  def open(port, user_options) do\n   102→    default_options = []\n   103→\n   104→    resolved_options = Core.merge_options(@hardcoded_options ++ default_options, user_options)\n   105→\n   106→    Core.open_socket(port, resolved_options)\n   107→  end\n   108→\n   109→  # Delegate all other transport operations to Core\n   110→\n   111→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   112→  @spec controlling_process(Abyss.Transport.socket(), pid()) ::\n   113→          Abyss.Transport.on_controlling_process()\n   114→  defdelegate controlling_process(socket, pid), to: Core\n   115→\n   116→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   117→  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer(), timeout()) ::\n   118→          Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n   119→  defdelegate recv(socket, length, timeout), to: Core\n   120→\n   121→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   122→  @spec send(Abyss.Transport.socket(), iodata()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_send()\n   123→  defdelegate send(socket, data), to: Core\n   124→  defdelegate send(socket, dest, data), to: Core\n   125→  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, data), to: Core\n   126→  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, anc_data, data), to: Core\n   127→\n   128→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   129→  @spec getopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_get_options()) ::\n   130→          Abyss.Transport.on_getopts()\n   131→  defdelegate getopts(socket, options), to: Core\n   132→\n   133→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   134→  @spec setopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_set_options()) ::\n   135→          Abyss.Transport.on_setopts()\n   136→  defdelegate setopts(socket, options), to: Core\n   137→\n   138→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   139→  @spec close(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) :: :ok\n   140→  defdelegate close(socket), to: Core\n   141→\n   142→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   143→  @spec sockname(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) ::\n   144→          Abyss.Transport.on_sockname()\n   145→  defdelegate sockname(socket), to: Core\n   146→\n   147→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   148→  @spec peername(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_peername()\n   149→  defdelegate peername(socket), to: Core\n   150→\n   151→  @impl Abyss.Transport\n   152→  @spec getstat(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.socket_stats()\n   153→  defdelegate getstat(socket), to: Core\n   154→\n   155→  @doc \"\"\"\n   156→  Utility function to send a message and receive a response.\n   157→\n   158→  This is useful for client-side unicast request/response patterns.\n   159→\n   160→  ## Parameters\n   161→  - `{ip, port}` - Remote endpoint to send to\n   162→  - `data` - Data to send\n   163→  - `timeout` - Receive timeout in milliseconds (default: 5000)\n   164→\n   165→  ## Returns\n   166→  - `{:ok, recv_data}` - Successfully received response\n   167→  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to send or receive\n   168→\n   169→  ## Examples\n   170→\n   171→      iex> Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast.send_recv({{1, 1, 1, 1}, 53}, dns_query, 5000)\n   172→      {:ok, {{1, 1, 1, 1}, 53, dns_response}}\n   173→  \"\"\"\n   174→  @spec send_recv(\n   175→          {Abyss.Transport.address(), :inet.port_number()},\n   176→          iodata(),\n   177→          timeout()\n   178→        ) :: Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n   179→  def send_recv({ip, port}, data, timeout \\\\ 5000) do\n   180→    case open(0, mode: :binary, active: false) do\n   181→      {:ok, socket} ->\n   182→        try do\n   183→          :ok = Core.send(socket, ip, port, data)\n   184→          Core.recv(socket, 0, timeout)\n   185→        after\n   186→          Core.close(socket)\n   187→        end\n   188→\n   189→      {:error, reason} ->\n   190→        {:error, reason}\n   191→    end\n   192→  end\n   193→end\n   194→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7KgdziV3oR5cphfXQGxxs","session_id":"50d21925-9237-40c9-8dd2-03e5c6e35c37","uuid":"264d97b8-5ca8-4aca-a722-5e800ddf172a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/abyss/lib/abyss/transport/udp/unicast.ex","content":"defmodule Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  UDP transport implementation for unicast traffic.\n\n  This transport is optimized for standard unicast UDP communication patterns,\n  such as DNS queries, DHCP unicast messages, and other point-to-point UDP protocols.\n\n  ## Characteristics\n\n  - Socket configured with `active: false` for passive receive mode\n  - Broadcast disabled (`broadcast: false`)\n  - Optimized for request/response patterns\n  - Multiple listener processes for load distribution\n  - Connection pooling support\n\n  ## Usage\n\n  ```elixir\n  Abyss.start_link([\n    transport_module: Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast,\n    handler_module: MyUnicastHandler,\n    port: 53,\n    num_listeners: 50\n  ])\n  ```\n\n  ## Handler Requirements\n\n  Handlers used with this transport should implement the `Abyss.Handler` behaviour\n  and be designed for unicast request/response patterns.\n\n  ## Default Options\n\n  The following default options are set for unicast traffic:\n  - `mode: :binary` - Binary mode for data\n  - `reuseaddr: true` - Allow address reuse\n  - `reuseport: true` - Allow port reuse across listeners\n  - `active: false` - Passive receive mode\n  - `broadcast: false` - Disable broadcast\n\n  ## See Also\n\n  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP.Broadcast` - For broadcast/multicast traffic\n  - `Abyss.Transport.UDP` - Original unified UDP transport (backward compatibility)\n  \"\"\"\n\n  @behaviour Abyss.Transport\n\n  alias Abyss.Transport.UDP.Core\n\n  @hardcoded_options [\n    mode: :binary,\n    reuseaddr: true,\n    reuseport: true,\n    active: false,\n    broadcast: false\n  ]\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Creates and returns a listener socket for unicast UDP traffic.\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `port` - The UDP port to listen on\n  - `user_options` - Additional socket options provided by the user\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `{:ok, socket}` - Successfully opened socket\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to open socket\n\n  ## Examples\n\n      iex> Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast.listen(5353, [ip: {0, 0, 0, 0}])\n      {:ok, #Port<0.1234>}\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec listen(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_listen()\n  def listen(port, user_options) do\n    default_options = []\n\n    resolved_options = Core.merge_options(@hardcoded_options ++ default_options, user_options)\n\n    Core.open_socket(port, resolved_options)\n  end\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Opens a UDP socket for sending unicast traffic.\n\n  This is typically used for creating client sockets.\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `port` - The local UDP port (use 0 for any available port)\n  - `user_options` - Additional socket options\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `{:ok, socket}` - Successfully opened socket\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to open socket\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec open(:inet.port_number(), [:inet.inet_backend() | :gen_udp.open_option()]) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_open()\n  def open(port, user_options) do\n    default_options = []\n\n    resolved_options = Core.merge_options(@hardcoded_options ++ default_options, user_options)\n\n    Core.open_socket(port, resolved_options)\n  end\n\n  # Delegate all other transport operations to Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec controlling_process(Abyss.Transport.socket(), pid()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_controlling_process()\n  defdelegate controlling_process(socket, pid), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec recv(Abyss.Transport.socket(), non_neg_integer(), timeout()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n  defdelegate recv(socket, length, timeout), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec send(Abyss.Transport.socket(), iodata()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_send()\n  defdelegate send(socket, data), to: Core\n  defdelegate send(socket, dest, data), to: Core\n  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, data), to: Core\n  defdelegate send(socket, ip, port, anc_data, data), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec getopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_get_options()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_getopts()\n  defdelegate getopts(socket, options), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec setopts(Abyss.Transport.socket(), Abyss.Transport.socket_set_options()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_setopts()\n  defdelegate setopts(socket, options), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec close(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) :: :ok\n  defdelegate close(socket), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec sockname(Abyss.Transport.socket() | Abyss.Transport.listener_socket()) ::\n          Abyss.Transport.on_sockname()\n  defdelegate sockname(socket), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec peername(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.on_peername()\n  defdelegate peername(socket), to: Core\n\n  @impl Abyss.Transport\n  @spec getstat(Abyss.Transport.socket()) :: Abyss.Transport.socket_stats()\n  defdelegate getstat(socket), to: Core\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Utility function to send a message and receive a response.\n\n  This is useful for client-side unicast request/response patterns.\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `{ip, port}` - Remote endpoint to send to\n  - `data` - Data to send\n  - `timeout` - Receive timeout in milliseconds (default: 5000)\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `{:ok, recv_data}` - Successfully received response\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to send or receive\n\n  ## Examples\n\n      iex> Abyss.Transport.UDP.Unicast.send_recv({{1, 1, 1, 1}, 53}, dns_query, 5000)\n      {:ok, {{1, 1, 1, 1}, 53, dns_response}}\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec send_recv(\n          {Abyss.Transport.address(), :inet.port_number()},\n          iodata(),\n          timeout()\n        ) :: Abyss.Transport.on_recv()\n  def send_recv({ip, port}, data, timeout \\\\ 5000) do\n    case open(0, mode: :binary, active: false) do\n      {:ok, socket} ->\n        try do\n          :ok = Core.send(socket, ip, port, data)\n          Core.recv(socket, 0, timeout)\n        after\n          Core.close(socket)\n        end\n\n      {:error, reason} ->\n        {:error, reason}\n    end\n  end\nend\n","numLines":194,"startLine":1,"totalLines":194}}}
